WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.240
Every mortgage transaction rests on two documents,
and they do different jobs.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.800
The promissory note is the promise to
repay — it creates the debt.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:15.500
The security instrument, the deed of trust,
pledges the property as security for that debt.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.720
On this note, let's locate the terms
that make it enforceable.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.110
This is the promissory note behind our running example.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.550
It's organized in numbered sections.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.960
Section 1 states the promise to pay.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.890
Section 2 sets the interest rate.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.790
Section 3 sets the payment amount and schedule.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.690
Further down, Section 5 covers the late charge —

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:48.490
and on page two, Section 9 names the deed of
trust as this note's security instrument.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.910
Locate Section 1, the Borrower's Promise to Pay.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:57.450
The borrower promises to pay the lender, ResiCentral,
LLC, a principal of $1,189,150 —

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.000
the amount actually borrowed, plus interest.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.770
Section 2 sets the interest rate:
7.5 percent per year.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:15.000
On a fixed-rate note like this one, that rate
applies both before and after any default.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.840
Section 3 sets the payment terms: a monthly payment
of $8,314.71, due the first of each month,
beginning September 1, 2026.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:31.000
If any balance remains on the Maturity Date,
August 1, 2056, it's due in full that day.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.710
Section 5 is the late charge.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:41.840
Miss the fifteen-day grace period, and the borrower owes
5 percent of the overdue principal and interest payment —

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.000
charged once per late payment, not compounding.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.080
Turn to Section 9, Secured Note, on page two.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:57.980
It names the deed of trust, dated the same day
as this note, as the security instrument that protects
the lender if these promises aren't kept.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:05.160
This is the link back to where we started: the
note is the debt, the deed of trust is the remedy.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.820
Every note earns its terms by section, not by feel:

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.470
principal, rate, payment, and maturity in
Sections 1 through 3,

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.670
the late charge in Section 5,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:18.320
and the link to the security instrument in Section 9.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.250
Read it that way, and the second document —

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:26.000
the deed of trust — will already make sense
before you turn to it.
